 "prose": {
  "guidance": "This corpus is numismatic scholarship on the coinage of Alexander the Great and the\nHellenistic kingdoms: hoard reports, mint studies and dating arguments. A hoard is a\ngroup of coins buried together and found together; the books name a hoard by where and\nwhen it was found (the Demanhur hoard, Asia Minor 1964, Armenak) and the later books add\nan IGCH number. A hoard report lists the coins the hoard held, mint by mint. Each coin or\ngroup of coins is a thing in its own right: the ruler in whose name it was struck, the\nmint that struck it, its denomination (stater, tetradrachm, drachm), metal, weight, when\nit was struck, and the hoard it came from when the text says so. A mint is a city where\ncoins were struck. A ruler is the king or dynast named on the coin. Attributions are\nclaims a scholar makes about a coin's mint or date, graded by the kind of evidence\noffered; a later book may dispute an earlier attribution, and the dispute is worth\nsurfacing.\n",
